1
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
The true story of an American ninja.

2
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Bloodsport is a action, thriller, drama film directed by Newt Arnold.

3
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:06,000
An American Army Major goes AWOL to Hong Kong for an
outlawed martial arts contest called the Kumite.

4
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:06,000
Frank Dux, played by Jean-Claude Van Damme.

5
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Chong Li, played by Bolo Yeung Sze.

6
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Ray Jackson, played by Donald Gibb.

7
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
Janice Kent, played by Leah Ayres.

8
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:06,000
Helmer, played by Norman Burton.

9
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:06,000
Rawlins, played by Forest Whitaker.

10
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Bloodsport is a 1988 American martial arts film directed by Newt Arnold and starring Jean-Claude Van Damme, with a
supporting cast of Leah Ayres, Donald Gibb, Roy Chiao, Norman Burton, Forest Whitaker, and Bolo Yeung.

11
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:06,000
The film centers on Frank Dux (Van Damme), a United States Army Captain and ninjutsu practitioner who
competes in an underground full-contact martial arts tournament called the Kumite in Hong Kong.

12
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:06,000
Based on Dux's real-life claims, the film was marketed as a true story.

13
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:06,000
It was one of Van Damme's first lead roles and showcased his
abilities, launching his career as a mainstream action star.

14
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
The screenplay is based on many of Dux's claims first covered in
the November 1980 issue of Black Belt magazine.

15
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
The real Dux served as the action choreographer and technical advisor.

16
01:04:00,000 --> 01:04:06,000
After its release, many of Dux's claims were disputed, including by co-screenwriter Sheldon Lettich,
who claimed Dux fabricated his fight record and the very existence of the Kumite.

17
01:08:00,000 --> 01:08:06,000
Bloodsport was produced and released by Cannon Films on February 26, 1988.

18
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
Despite negative critical reviews, it was a considerable box office
success, grossing $50 million on a budget of $1.5–2.3 million.

19
01:16:00,000 --> 01:16:06,000
The success of the film helped the resurgence of the martial arts film genre in America, and
has been credited with influencing the development of mixed martial arts.

20
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
It has also been identified as a cult classic and spawned a film series
which includes four sequels, without Van Damme's involvement.

21
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
The film explores themes of martial arts, sports, biography, fistfight, sensei.

22
01:28:00,000 --> 01:28:06,000
The tone is emotional and intense.
